WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.640
- Today we're going to
create an avatar that looks

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.989
and moves like me.

00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:04.822
(camera snapping)

00:00:04.822 --> 00:00:07.251
We begin by smiling into the camera.

00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:09.300
(dramatic music)
I love to smile.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.920
Smiling's my favorite.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.173
I'm breathing gently for a short second.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.701
Is this what breathing looks like?

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:18.330
(dramatic music)

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.243
I'm Joanna Stern, and I'm
excited to host this video.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.170
No, I am the real Joanna.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.150
Okay, so I cloned myself.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.430
Kinda. Howdy.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.263
Why?

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.550
Well, the latest AI
tools that generate text

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.190
and images already make it
hard to tell the difference

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.830
between what's real and what's fake.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.170
What's coming next with AI-generated voice

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.560
and video is gonna blur
the lines even more.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.450
So I came up with a challenge.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.200
Can I replace myself with AI for the day?

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.660
Yes, I came up with four challenges to see

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.780
if AI me could sub in for real me

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.742
so real me had more time for me things.

00:00:57.742 --> 00:01:00.420
(tranquil music)

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.850
Or at least that's how I wanted it to go.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:03.683
- Still a little creeped out

00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.750
that I'm looking at a frozen Joanna.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.580
- Okay, let's do this.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.637
- Scene three, take two, calibration.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:11.470
(board clapping)

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:12.360
- Before we get into the challenges,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.250
let's talk about my AI avatar,

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.533
which was made by a
startup called Synthesia.

00:01:17.664 --> 00:01:18.780
Going to make my avatar.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.610
At a professional studio in New York,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.180
the company recorded me doing
a series of head movements.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.310
I feel like I'm at the eye doctor. Okay.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.550
And reading through a rather
odd pre-written script.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.260
Positive thinking will help you believe

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.590
in your self and fill you with
self-esteem and confidence.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.810
After that, I headed to an audio studio

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.900
where I recorded another
script for about an hour.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.810
My name is Joanna Stern
and I hereby consent

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.140
to this audio recording
to create a custom voice.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.170
The company took that all
and used it as training data

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.019
and ran it through their
AI neural networks.

00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:55.590
(dramatic music)
(text buzzing)

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.970
Hello, Joanna.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.650
You don't mind if I call you Joanna?

00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.483
Do you?

00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:01.110
Okay. So The voice isn't the best.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.840
A tool called ElevenLabs
produced something better

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.180
after my producer Kenny uploaded two hours

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.220
of my previous recordings.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.480
I am the real Joanna.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:10.740
I am the real Joanna.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.090
I am the real Joanna.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.730
Both Synthesia and
ElevenLabs work similarly.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.510
Type in anything and AI Joanna
just says it right back.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.040
Synthesia is aimed at companies

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.320
that want to make internal videos.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.740
It charges at least $1,000
to create a custom avatar.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:26.760
Creating a voice clone

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.010
with ElevenLabs is $5 a month.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.680
Challenge one: phone calls.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.300
I happened to have a
call scheduled that day

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.880
with Evan Spiegel, the CEO of Snap.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.340
The company recently released My AI,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.350
a chatbot within the popular app.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.970
Hey Evan, it's Joanna.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.980
Do you worry that if we
chat with AI all day,

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.930
we'll stop talking to our real friends?

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.270
- [Evan] Definitely not
what we've been seeing.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.830
I think that's one of the real benefits

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.050
of our sort of testing
and learning approach.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.400
So far, I think if anything,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.800
it's gonna become a
conversation enhancement

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.560
and improve the way
that people communicate

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.610
with their friends and family.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.400
- Did you think by any
chance that my question

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.683
to you was generated by an AI voice?

00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:05.516
(Evan chuckling)

00:03:05.516 --> 00:03:06.510
- [Evan] No. No.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:11.010
I mean, the first word or two
was a little bit of a giveaway

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.288
but I thought maybe you
were extra serious today.

00:03:13.288 --> 00:03:14.121
(Joanna chuckling)

00:03:14.121 --> 00:03:15.450
- [Joanna] Even my own
sister was pretty fooled

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.134
when I called her about her dead fish.

00:03:18.134 --> 00:03:18.967
- [Julia] Hello?

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:19.800
- Hey, Jules.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.030
I just heard about Swimmy Dimi

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.850
and I wanted to let you know
how sorry I am for your loss.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.380
Did you think it was me?

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.030
- [Julia] At first, yes.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:27.863
And then no.

00:03:27.863 --> 00:03:31.380
Like it sounds, it's obviously
exactly like you, but just

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.530
with the fact that like, it
doesn't pause for talking back.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.200
- Challenge one: pass.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.140
Challenge two: create a TikTok.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.350
I asked ChatGPT to write a TikTok script

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.550
in the voice of Joanna Stern
about an obscure iOS 16 tip.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.850
The hardest thing was getting
ChatGPT to write the truth.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.443
It just made stuff up.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.530
Finally, I got a good one.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.230
Although the writing
certainly was not very me.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.240
I pasted the script into Synthesia,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.450
put a green screen behind
my avatar and exported it.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.419
While the WSJ TikTok team edited, I.

00:04:06.419 --> 00:04:11.010
(pleasant piano music)
(Joanna snoring)

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.260
I was pretty impressed
with the final TikTok.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.650
TikTok fam, it's Joanna
Stern, your iOS wizard.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.660
Today we're unearthing the hidden world

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.493
of back tap gestures.

00:04:19.493 --> 00:04:21.853
I love that I did not have to shoot this.

00:04:21.853 --> 00:04:23.850
I did not have to put on nice clothes,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.450
do my hair, do my makeup, say these lines.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.150
But TikTok was less impressed.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.140
They picked up on the fact

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.390
that the avatar never moves its arms,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.360
that the mouth movements
don't always match the audio

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.820
and that there's little facial expression.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.010
Synthesia has already
started to improve a lot

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.740
of this in beta versions of its avatars.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.553
- Look, I can nod my head.

00:04:45.553 --> 00:04:47.370
(dramatic music)

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.010
- Challenge two: fail.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.040
Challenge three: bank biometrics.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.080
Instead of asking security questions,

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.570
some banks use your
voice to confirm it's you

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.540
before transferring you
to a customer service rep.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.880
- [System] This call will
be monitored and recorded

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.880
and your voice may be
used for verification.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.400
Please speak your first and last name,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.680
followed by your mailing address.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.003
- Joanna Stern.
(beeping sound)

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.880
- [Nikki] This is Nikki with
Chase credit card services.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.080
- It worked.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.660
Chase confirmed the voice
and put me straight through

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:22.770
to a service rep.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.960
No additional questions asked.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.600
Later in the day, I asked
our intern Slav to try

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.191
to do his best impression of
me to see what would happen.

00:05:31.191 --> 00:05:33.452
- [System] Please speak
your first and last name,

00:05:33.452 --> 00:05:35.183
followed by your mailing address.

00:05:35.183 --> 00:05:36.172
(beeping sound)

00:05:36.172 --> 00:05:39.090
- Joanna Stern.
(beeping sound)

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.280
- [System] Please enter the
last three digits printed

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.860
on the signature panel
on the back of your card.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.150
- See, in Slav's case,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.340
the voice biometric system didn't buy it.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.770
It asked for further verification.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.807
When I reached out to
Chase, a spokeswoman said,

00:05:52.807 --> 00:05:55.410
"We use voice biometrics,
along with a variety

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.560
of other methods to authenticate
customers who call us."

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.270
She added that to complete requests,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.390
customers must provide
additional information.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.940
Challenge three: pass.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.880
Challenge four: video calls.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.880
I asked ChatGPT to generate
some generic meeting phrases

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.580
and exported videos of
my avatar saying them.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.140
Then I installed some software

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.650
on my Mac to pump that video
into my Google Meet calls.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:20.880
That sounds good.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.124
- [Caller 1] Oh, you're muted, Joanna.

00:06:23.124 --> 00:06:26.130
My God, is this the real Joanna?

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.200
- Yeah, this looks like a fake.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.040
It sounds good.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.730
- She looks, yeah, what is happening here?

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.740
- How did you know that it wasn't me?

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.890
- It looked like a
hologram version of you.

00:06:37.899 --> 00:06:38.732
- [Caller 2] It was the posture for me.

00:06:39.961 --> 00:06:42.060
- She also didn't make any jokes.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.953
- Challenge four: big fail.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.280
So what did we learn today?

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.540
We learned that video clones

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.340
aren't going to fool anyone yet

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.590
but AI voices are quite good.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.620
We also learned that while you
could use these to save time,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.780
people could also misuse them.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.420
Do I wanna avoid going
to the studio some days?

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.253
Yep.

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:04.140
Do I fear scammers using
our voices to call banks

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.760
or our families?

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:06.630
Yep.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.000
Synthesia says it requires
those creating avatars

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.560
to give verbal consent.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.840
ElevenLabs requires you
check a box saying you

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.970
have permission to use the voice

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:18.030
and the company says it's
capable of identifying its voices

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.950
if they are misused.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.080
Either way, it means we're
all going to have to be

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.370
on high alert to tell
the real versus the AI.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.983
- And finally, stay human, everyone.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.350
Good luck. I am inevitable.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:33.538
(fingers snapping)

